</figure>
<div id="Ax3.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax3.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#Ax3.T6" title="In -F Comparison to other Multi-View losses ‣ Experimental Details ‣ A Principled Framework for Multi-View Contrastive Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI</span></a> and <a href="#Ax3.T7" title="In -F Comparison to other Multi-View losses ‣ Experimental Details ‣ A Principled Framework for Multi-View Contrastive Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VII</span></a>, we observe that MV-InfoNCE outperforms MV-CL1, which has the same negative index set, while MV-DHEL also performs better than MV-CL2, which shares the same negative index set, on both the demanding ImageNet-100 dataset and in the multimodal setup. We argue that this performance degradation originates from both MV-CL1 and MV-CL2 violating <span id="Ax3.SS6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0064C8;">P3</span> by using multiple terms per data point.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</article>
</div>
